Well, I'm doing it correctly then, though I'm
limited in how often I can do it by the threat of
starvation if I can't make a food prayer. The
responses are few and far between, so far the _only_
useful response has been Anhur blessing Magic Bane.

Often, my last sacrifice before I'm forced to pray
for food by having none and being "fainting" is
still getting only "a hopeful feeling" in response,
so I know I'm frequently at the ragged edge of
offending Anhur when I pray.

My current technique is only to pray for a blessing
other than food if I have at least one (death
dropped) food ration, to eat all the early kills
(except noxious stuff like bats, which I sacrifice
instead) after a prior prayer to try to increase my
food timeout, and to sacrifice the stuff killed
longer after a prayer, when there is a better chance
of it having a positive response.

Then, when I get hungry, if I've still only seen
"hopeful feeling", I start alternating eating kills
until not hungry, and sacrificing kills while not
hungry, until the random sparseness of kills finds
me either 45 turns weak, or fainting, when I do a
food prayer.

This isn't like pudding farming, where food is in
instant and near infinite supply. With only one
random monster on average generated per 50 turns,
only a third of those dropping corpses among the
ones eligible to drop corpses, and none of the rest
doing do, with the nutrition of most corpses quite
inferior to "real food", with many dropped corpses
too acidic, confusing, or hallucinatory to eat (I
play my wizards as orcs just so "poisonous" isn't in
this list) and with a balancing act between eating
corpses and sacrificing them, this kind of "farming"
is always done in a death march along the brink of
starvation -- it's lots of fun, but the odds are
badly against me, since just one of those low
probability, excruciatingly long prayer timeouts can
be the end of my PC.

Also, in distant parts of the dungeon, closed rooms
clotted up with nymphs, leprechauns, piercers, and
lichens keep edible monsters far away from the PC by
blocking travel.

Even more than a ring of slow digestion, I'd like to
see some death dropped means of teleporting monsters
away (a wand, but better a spellbook if there is
such a thing), so that I could send my current
carefully preserved mining squad of two rock moles
to the distant parts of the dungeon level to tunnel
their way back, and in the process, open paths for
other, delicious monsters. Instead, Tunneler and
StarNose are orbiting around the altar, and there
are no walls left of the room where it sits, only a
remnant door frame, and the surrounding rock (I'm in
a lower right corner of the map again, by
coincidence) has been cleared to the edge of the
map on two sides of the room, and some unknown
distance toward the center.  [Having gotten YASDed
my last try at this for stepping ten or twelve steps
away from the altar, I'm hovering my PC like a moth
near a flame around the altar this time.]

I've received a blessed smoky potion via death
drop, too, but like everyone else who finds a wish
source long before kitting up much at all, am
reluctant to expend a possible wish on something I
might find at any minute once I give up altar
squatting and begin my downward YASD search, so it
is just in a stash on one of the three Elbereth-ed
squares next to the altar.

Another annoyance is the relative sparseness of
daggers so far. I have lots of variety, among
orcish, regular, and runed, cursed, blessed, and
uncursed, but that means my biggest stack that I can
put into my quiver is only 3 deep, not enough even
at expert in daggars to start using daggers as
thrown weapons when I dare not step away from the
altar to retrieve them until all dangerous monsters
in sight are dead. The area just now is awash with
giant ants, though not yet soldier ants, and those
beasts are much faster than wizard Orc Kibble, who
with an AC of -2 isn't yet proof against their
attacks. I'll be a lot happier when at least a
couple gnomes with large daggar arsenels drop dead
on the altar.

